- $ \sin(y + z) = \sin\left(\frac{2\pi}{3}\right) = \sqrt{3}/2 $
- $ \sin x = \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{3}\right) = \sqrt{3}/2 $
- $ \sin z = \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{3}\right) = \sqrt{3}/2 $

So:
$$
\frac{\sin(x + y) \sin(y + z)}{\sin x \sin z} = \frac{(\sqrt{3}/2)^2}{(\sqrt{3}/2)^2} = 1
